-- title:
--   Germ of the kernel ideal equals kernel of the stalk map
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ and $Y$ be schemes, let $\sigma \colon Y \to X$ be a morphism which is a closed immersion, let $y$ be a point of $Y$, let $U$ be an affine open subscheme of $X$, and suppose the image $\sigma(y)$ of $y$ under the underlying continuous map of $\sigma$ lies in $U$. Write $\sigma.\mathrm{ker}$ for the kernel ideal sheaf of $\sigma$ on $X$, so that its ideal of sections over $U$ is the kernel of $\sigma^\sharp \colon \Gamma(X, U) \to \Gamma(Y, \sigma^{-1}U)$, and let $\mathrm{germ} \colon \Gamma(X,U) \to \mathcal{O}_{X,\sigma(y)}$ be the germ map of the structure presheaf of $X$ at the point $\sigma(y)$ of $U$. The assertion is an equality of ideals of the local ring $\mathcal{O}_{X,\sigma(y)}$: the ideal generated by the image under this germ map of the ideal $(\sigma.\mathrm{ker})(U)$ coincides with the kernel of the ring homomorphism $\mathcal{O}_{X,\sigma(y)} \to \mathcal{O}_{Y,y}$ induced by $\sigma$ on stalks. Thus the ideal sheaf of the closed subscheme, extended from sections over an affine open to the stalk, is exactly the kernel of the corresponding map of local rings.
--
--   This is the standard comparison between the ideal sheaf of a closed subscheme and the kernels of the induced surjections of local rings, in the form needed to pass from sections over an affine open to stalks. It is used to identify the stalk of the kernel ideal sheaf at a point (for instance as the maximal ideal at a point with field-valued residue), in the compatibility of kernels of stalk maps with pullbacks along surjections, and in the analysis of the ideal cutting out a point of the modular curve $X_1(p)$ in its two-chart model.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicGeometry_Scheme_Hom_map_germ_ker_ideal_eq_ker_stalkMap.lean

